measures. They have been one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stand out for the 2nd highest pre-snap motion rate and the most backs in the backfield in the league. Justin Herbert has run the passing game, with Tre Harris leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Chargers defense has been about average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been takeaways per game, where it ranks 8th, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 26th. Tuli Tuipulotu has led the way with 3 sacks. Their scheme has been fairly conventional, and the closest thing to a signature is a number of defenders in the box that ranks 6th lowest in the league.","special":"The Chargers have been one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 17% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers have made 90% of field goals, which is a little above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the quarterback, about 24% of the money on the books. Their linebackers were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Justin Herbert has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 1,720 yards. Targets have been spread around, with Tre Harris and Quentin Johnston leading a group where nobody has more than a fifth of the looks. Omarion Hampton has handled most of the work in the backfield with 57% of the carries. On defense, Tuli Tuipulotu leads the team with 3 sacks and Cam Hart has picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"Mike McDaniel is calling the offense and Chris O'Leary is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2025 Dolphins under Mike McDaniel and the 2025 Ravens under Todd Monken. Their defense looks most like the 2025 Seahawks under Mike Macdonald and the 2023 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 25th in EPA per play in 2025 to 32nd so far this season, a modest decline. The defense moved from 7th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 18th so far this season, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was pass rate over expected, which went from 0.64 to -9.79.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}